  <!-- SPIRITUAL DIRECTION GUIDE -->
  <div class="guide-cover">
    <div class="guide-title">Present Everywhere, Known Nowhere</div>
    <div class="guide-subtitle">When showing up has become a substitute for being seen</div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">👁</span><span class="guide-section-label">What Your Scores Are Saying</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p>Your scores indicate a person who has learned to function exceptionally well in the visible layers of life — and who is quietly starving in the layers no one sees. Relationally, you are present in most of the key contexts of your life. You show up. You contribute. You maintain. But the data tells a more honest story underneath: Being Known scores at 46%, Having a Voice at 41%, Trust & Safety at 44%. These are not numbers about your relationships — they are numbers about what you let people see of you inside those relationships. And the picture they paint is of someone who has become very good at being in rooms without being truly in them.</p>
      <p>Your Heart & Soul scores sharpen that picture considerably. Grief sits at 38%, Anger at 36%, Shame at 42%. These three together are the most common interior pattern in high-functioning disconnection — a person who has learned to manage what they feel rather than move through it. The grief hasn't been processed. The anger has been suppressed rather than named. The shame has been covered rather than exposed. And so the interior life runs quietly in the background, shaping everything without being acknowledged by anyone — including yourself.</p>
      <p>What's notable is your Self score at 67% — you are more self-aware than most. You know something is happening underneath. You can probably name it better than most people could. And yet that awareness has not yet translated into movement. Knowing and doing remain separated by something — likely fear (44%) and the long habit of carrying things alone. Your Desire & Hope score at 52% tells you something important: there is still wanting in you. The heart hasn't gone quiet. It is waiting for permission to be honest.</p>
      <p>The God score at 43% is the piece that pulls everything together. This is not a story about relational skill or personal failure. It is a story about a person who has not yet found a place — not with people, not with God, not even fully with themselves — where it is truly safe to be undone. That is the real picture your scores are painting, and it is worth sitting with honestly.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">🌊</span><span class="guide-section-label">What's Happening Underneath</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p>These scores point to a pattern that develops slowly and almost invisibly in people who are capable, conscientious, and quietly self-sufficient. Somewhere along the way — probably early, probably in response to something that required you to hold yourself together — you learned that showing up was safer than being seen. That competence was a more reliable currency than vulnerability. That the cost of being truly known was higher than the cost of carrying things alone.</p>
      <p>This pattern doesn't feel like a wound from the inside. It feels like wisdom. It feels like emotional intelligence. It feels like knowing when to speak and when to stay quiet, when to lean in and when to hold back. But the data underneath tells a different story. The grief at 38% is not about the absence of hard things — it's about hard things that were survived rather than grieved. The anger at 36% is not about the absence of things worth being angry about — it's about anger that got converted into forward motion rather than processed as honest feeling. Something keeps getting managed that was meant to be felt.</p>
      <p>The shame pattern is worth particular attention. A score of 42% doesn't mean you are drowning in shame — it means shame is operating quietly in the background, shaping how much of yourself you offer to people. There is likely a version of you that no one has fully seen. Not because you are hiding something terrible, but because the risk of being fully known has never felt worth taking. The self-aware part of you (67%) knows this is true. The fearful part (44%) keeps choosing safety instead.</p>
      <p>What's being managed here is not a behavioral problem. It is a soul problem. The interior life has been running in maintenance mode for a long time — functional, competent, and quietly lonely. What it needs is not more self-improvement. It needs a place where it doesn't have to perform.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">✦</span><span class="guide-section-label">Where God Seems to Be Moving</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p>God's invitation in this season is not subtle. It is showing up in the gaps — in the low Anger score, in the unprocessed Grief, in the 43% relationship with God that says something has gone quiet between you. The invitation is this: stop managing your interior life and start bringing it. Not to therapy, not to a program, not to a system — but to God, who is not waiting for you to have it together before He shows up.</p>
      <p>The specific door God seems to be opening is the one you've kept most carefully closed — the door to being honestly, messily, unproductively present with Him. Not devotional presence. Not performance presence. The kind of presence where you say what is actually true: that you are tired, that you are angry about things you haven't named out loud, that you carry grief you haven't finished, that you are not sure He has been showing up the way you needed Him to. That conversation — the one you haven't had — is the one He is waiting for.</p>
      <p>Your Desire & Hope score at 52% is the signal that something in you still believes this is possible. The heart is not closed. It is cautious. And the invitation of this season is to bring that cautious, half-believing, honestly unsure heart to God and let Him meet it where it actually is — not where it thinks it should be. That is where the movement begins.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">🧭</span><span class="guide-section-label">What Soul Care Offers You</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p>Soul care attends to what has been neglected. For someone with your scores, what has been neglected is not your relationships — you are present in most of them. What has been neglected is the interior life underneath those relationships. The grief that hasn't finished. The anger that got converted into forward motion. The shame that keeps you offering people a curated version of yourself. Soul care creates a space where those things can finally surface — not to be fixed, but to be named honestly in the presence of someone who can hold them with you.</p>
      <p>Spiritual direction is not therapy. It is not coaching. It is not accountability. It is the practice of sitting with someone who helps you notice what you've been too busy or too guarded to see — where God is present, where He is absent, what your interior life is actually telling you, and what honest next steps look like. Given what your scores reveal, what that kind of attention could do is significant: it could give the grief somewhere to go. It could give the anger a name. It could give the shame something to stand against. And it could give your relationship with God the honesty it has been waiting for.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">→</span><span class="guide-section-label">Your Next Honest Step</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p>The Discovery Call is a free 15-minute conversation with Steve to look at your complete picture together. It is not a sales call. It is not a commitment to anything. It is simply an honest conversation about what your scores are actually telling you, what you're carrying, and whether working together makes sense. No pressure, no agenda beyond that.</p>
      <p>If what you've read in this guide resonates — if something in you recognized itself in these pages — that recognition is worth a conversation. You've been carrying this long enough to know it doesn't resolve on its own. The next honest step is a simple one: reach out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <div class="guide-section-hdr"><span class="guide-section-icon">✤</span><span class="guide-section-label">A Practice for This Season</span></div>
    <div class="guide-section-body">
      <p><strong style="color:#f0e8df">The Daily Examen</strong><br>
      The Examen is a 15-minute prayer practice developed by Ignatius of Loyola that trains you to notice where God was present in your day — and where you were present to God. You move through your day in reverse, asking two questions: Where did I feel most alive, most connected, most like myself? And where did I feel most drained, most absent, most like I was going through motions? You do not try to fix anything. You simply notice. Given your scores on Being Seen (58%) and your God score (43%), this practice addresses the core gap directly — it builds the habit of paying attention to your own interior experience, and it invites God into the ordinary details of your day rather than keeping Him at devotional distance.</p>
      <p><strong style="color:#f0e8df">The Prayer of Lament</strong><br>
      Lament is the practice of bringing honest grief and anger to God — not wrapped in theological qualifications, but raw and direct. The Psalms are full of it: "How long, Lord? Will you forget me forever?" Your Grief score (38%) and Anger score (36%) together indicate that significant weight has been carried without being released. Lament gives it somewhere to go. The practice is simple: take one thing you are genuinely grieved or angry about — something you haven't said out loud — and say it to God, directly and without editing. Stay with it for 10 minutes. Don't resolve it. Don't theologize it. Just bring it. This practice is specifically chosen for you because the pattern in your data isn't absence of feeling — it's suppression of feeling. Lament creates a container for what has been held too long.</p>
      <p><strong style="color:#f0e8df">Confession to a Trusted Person</strong><br>
      Not confession in the religious sense — confession as the practice of saying something true about yourself to another person that you have not said before. Your Shame score (42%) and Being Known score (46%) together point to a person who is carrying more alone than anyone around them knows. The antidote to shame is not self-compassion — it is witnessed honesty. Choose one person you trust. Tell them one true thing about yourself that you have been protecting. It doesn't need to be dramatic. It just needs to be real. The research on shame is clear: shame loses its power in the presence of empathy. This practice is chosen specifically because your data shows a person who is self-aware enough to know what they are carrying — and who has not yet let anyone else carry it with them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-closing">
    <div class="guide-closing-label">A Note from Steve</div>
    <div class="guide-closing-text">What strikes me most about this picture is not the low scores — it's the combination of high self-awareness and continued isolation. You know yourself well. You know something needs to change. And you are still carrying most of it alone. That gap between knowing and moving is exactly where spiritual direction lives. I've sat with many people whose scores look like yours, and what I've seen consistently is that the movement they were looking for wasn't far away — it was one honest conversation away. I'd be glad to be part of that conversation if you're ready for it.</div>
  </div>

